 ],
 "literal": "Saline wasteland having spread, spurge cactus having grown high, / even in daytime hooting, with the horned owl of protruding teeth, / by the light of the funeral pyre, along with demoness women, / fear-inducing indeed is this mist-covered ancient crematorium, / where tears wept by heart-desiring lovers / extinguish the white ash of the bone-filled cremation ground; / having seen the end of everyone in this world, / having become everything to all of mankind, / it does not know anyone seeing its own end.",
 "translation": "The saline wasteland has spread wide, and spurge cactus has grown high. Even in the daytime, horned owls with protruding teeth hoot. By the light of the funeral pyre, along with demoness women, this mist-shrouded ancient crematorium is indeed terrifying. Here, the tears wept by beloved partners extinguish the white ash of the bone-filled cremation ground. This place, which has seen the end of everyone in this world and has become the final destination for all of mankind, never knows anyone who can see its own end.",
 "modern": "களர்நிலம் பரவி, கள்ளிச்செடிகள் வளர்ந்து, பகலிலும் அலறும் கூகையோடு, கோரப்பற்களை உடைய பேய்மகளிருடன், ஈமவிளக்கின் ஒளியில் விளங்கும் இந்த மேகம் சூழ்ந்த சுடுகாடு அச்சம் தருவதாகும். நெஞ்சில் நிறைந்த காதலர் அழுத கண்ணீர், எலும்புகள் நிறைந்த சுடுகாட்டின் வெண்ணீற்றை அணைக்கிறது. உலகத்து மக்கள் எல்லாரது முடிவையும் தான் கண்டு, மனிதகுலத்திற்கு எல்லாம் தானேயாகி நிற்கும் இந்தச் சுடுகாடு, தன் முடிவைக் காண்பாரை என்றுமே கண்டதில்லை.",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Kāñci tiṇai (transience of life) and Peruṅkāñci tuṟai. The poet Tāyaṅkaṇṇaṉār describes the terrifying nature of the ancient crematorium (mutukāṭu) to emphasize the fleeting nature of human existence. He paints a vivid picture of a desolate wasteland overgrown with cactus, where owls hoot even during the day, and ghoulish women gather around the light of funeral pyres. The emotional core of the poem is the image of grieving lovers whose tears extinguish the hot ashes of the pyre. Ultimately, the poet personifies the crematorium as an eternal entity that witnesses the end of every human being, yet remains immortal itself, as no one can ever witness its demise.",
